# evals.humancopilot.eu — the findings site. Tenant drop-in for the shared Caddy edge.
#
# The edge engine (base Caddyfile with the `hardened` snippet and `import /etc/caddy/sites/*.caddy`,
# firewall, Cloudflare origin lock, the deploy@ reload grant) is owned by podcast_scraper
# (its ADR-114). This file is ours: infra/deploy/install_vhosts.sh installs it into
# /etc/caddy/sites/, gates the merged config with `caddy adapt`, then reloads.
#
# Installed only once listed in enabled.txt, after DNS points the name at the box: a vhost for
# a name that does not resolve here makes Caddy retry ACME and burn Let's Encrypt's budget.
# __EVALS_PORT__ is rendered at deploy time (127.0.0.1:8096, the evals-nginx container), and
# __INSPECTOR_PORT__ (127.0.0.1:8097, evals-inspector): the catalog lives under /catalog/ on this
# host, and its API under /catalog/api/, which the inspector answers as /api/. __MCP_PORT__
# (127.0.0.1:8098, evals-mcp): the MCP server at /mcp.
evals.humancopilot.eu {
	import hardened
	handle /catalog/api/* {
		uri strip_prefix /catalog
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:__INSPECTOR_PORT__
	}
	# The MCP server: its endpoint and its health, nothing else (its /metrics stays on the loopback
	# port, for the box's collector). A named matcher: `handle` takes one path, and `handle /mcp
	# /mcp/*` fails `caddy adapt` (wrong argument count). A tool call is a few hundred bytes; a body
	# over 64 KB is refused here, before the server reads it (an independent review, 2026-10-29:
	# parallel 4 MiB bodies took the container past its memory limit).
	@mcp path /mcp /mcp/*
	handle @mcp {
		request_body {
			max_size 64KB
		}
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:__MCP_PORT__
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:__EVALS_PORT__
	}
}
